ABUNDANCE ENGINE — SAFE INSTRUMENTATION CHARTER

Pre‑Instantiation Hygiene for Sovereign Systems
CC0 — Public Domain

---

Purpose
This charter defines the only measurements permitted in an abundance system.  
Its function is to strengthen sovereignty, circulation, and self‑correction without introducing surveillance, hierarchy, shame, or coercion.

This document exists to prevent drift before deployment.

---

Core Rule (Non‑Negotiable)

> If a metric can be used to compare people, it does not belong.  

Metrics tune systems, never humans.

---

Scope
Only three domains may be instrumented:

1. Time Circulation (UBT)  
2. Resource Circulation (UPRR / WAT)  
3. Knowledge Viscosity (OSIIN)

All other measurement domains collapse into surveillance, hierarchy, or shame and are therefore prohibited.

---

1. TIME CIRCULATION (UBT)

What May Be Measured
(aggregate, non‑attributed, non‑comparative)

- Recognition Latency  
  Time between contribution → acknowledgment  

- Rest Enforcement Ratio  
  Percentage of cycles where rest occurred after contribution  

- Net Time Balance (NTB)  
  Whether the commons trends time‑positive or time‑negative  

What Is Explicitly Prohibited

- Individual productivity  
- Contribution volume by person  
- Rankings, quotas, performance comparisons  
- Identification of high or low contributors  

Rationale
Time metrics tune structural rhythm, not effort.  
If NTB dips, adjust structure, not people.

---

2. RESOURCE CIRCULATION (UPRR / WAT)

What May Be Measured
(resources only; never users)

- Liters/person/day (aggregate)  
- Days of storage at current draw  
- Downtime hours per period  
- Mean time to repair  
- Leak recurrence frequency  
- Source or aquifer trend (up / flat / down)  

What Is Explicitly Prohibited

- Attribution of fault  
- Usage policing  
- Compliance scoring  
- Individual consumption tracking  

Rationale
A failure in flow is a signal, not a sin.  
If a child can read the dashboard without fear, the metric is valid.

---

3. KNOWLEDGE VISCOSITY (OSIIN)

What May Be Measured
(fluidity, not authorship)

- Update Half‑Life  
  Time until a design or SOP is revised or simplified  

- Fork‑to‑Merge Ratio  
  Whether adaptations flow back into the commons  

- Instruction Thickness  
  Ease with which a newcomer can follow guidance  

Canonical Proxy Test

> “Can a 12‑year‑old explain how this works?”  

Yes → knowledge is flowing  
No → knowledge is calcifying

What Is Explicitly Prohibited

- Authorship metrics  
- Contribution counts  
- Expertise ranking  
- Reputation scores  

Rationale
Knowledge must thin over time, not accrete.

---

Pre‑Failure Vectors (Early‑Warning Shapes)
These describe structural drift, not human error.  
They are the only recognized early warnings.

1. Recognition Lag Creep  
   Acknowledgment slips from hours → days → weeks  

2. Maintenance Invisibility  
   Preventive care disappears until failures cluster  

3. Knowledge Calcification  
   SOPs thicken; newcomers repeatedly stall  

4. Resource Shame Emergence  
   Hesitation to request needs; informal backchannels appear  

Each vector has one response: tune the system, never correct individuals.

---

Instrumentation Veto
A proposed metric is invalid if it:

- Can be used to judge or compare people  
- Increases fear, silence, or self‑comparison  
- Requires attribution to function  
- Incentivizes performance over circulation  
- Cannot be explained to a child without anxiety  

Invalid metrics are not revised.  
They are removed.

---

Invariant

> Instrumentation exists to tell the system it is getting tired before the people do.  

Anything else is drift.

